<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gaius Maecenas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 70–8 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gaius Maecenas was a Roman statesman and friend of Augustus, descended from an Etruscan noble family of Arretium. He acted as Octavian's diplomatic agent and deputy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> during the civil wars, but he is best remembered as patron of poets such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horace">Horace</a> and Propertius. His name became a byword for a generous patron of the arts.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gaius-maecenas.jpg" alt="Gaius Maecenas" width="320" height="332" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gaius_Cilnius_Maecenas_Roman_Statesman_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gaius Cilnius Maecenas was a friend and political adviser to Octavian, who later reigned as the emperor Augustus. He prided himself on an ancient Etruscan lineage. He claimed descent from the princely house of the Cilnii of Arretium. Horace alludes to this in the opening of his Odes with the phrase &quot;atavis edite regibus&quot;, descendant of kings. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> calls him Cilnius Maecenas, and it is possible that Cilnius was his mother's family name, or that Maecenas was a cognomen. His wealth may have been partly inherited, but he owed his position to his connection with Augustus; despite his wealth and power he chose not to enter the Senate and remained of equestrian rank.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Maecenas first appears in 40 BCE, when Octavian employed him to arrange his marriage with Scribonia and to help negotiate the Treaty of Brundisium and the reconciliation with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a>. In 37 BCE he and Marcus Cocceius Nerva patched up a reconciliation between the two claimants to power by the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarentum">Tarentum</a>. During the Sicilian war against Sextus Pompeius in 36 BCE he held supreme administrative control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and he acted for Octavian again before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-actium">Battle of Actium</a>, when he crushed the conspiracy of Lepidus the Younger. In his last years he fell somewhat out of favour with Augustus. He died in 8 BCE, leaving Augustus sole heir to his wealth.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Maecenas is best known for supporting young poets, and in many languages his name means a patron of the arts, as in French mécène and German Mäzen. Virgil wrote the Georgics in his honour and introduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horace">Horace</a> to him in 38 BCE. Maecenas gave Horace financial support and an estate in the Sabine Mountains, and Propertius and Varius Rufus were also among his protégés. Some see the patronage as serving the state, reconciling minds to the new order. He wrote prose and verse himself, now lost apart from about twenty fragments, and Augustus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-people">Seneca</a> and Quintilian mocked his strange style. His gardens on the Esquiline Hill, the first of their kind in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, became imperial property after his death.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gaius Maecenas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Augustus made Etruria the seventh region of Italy, and Etruscan nobles such as his friend <b>Gaius Maecenas</b>, from an old family of Arretium, entered the Roman elite.</blockquote><a href="/culture/etruscans">Etruscans · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The city was dominated by great families, among them the Cilnii, whom Livy reports being driven out by a popular revolt in 302 BCE and restored with Roman help; the Augustan minister <b>Maecenas</b> was descended from them.</blockquote><a href="/state/arretium">Arretium</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arezzo">Arezzo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.46,11.88&amp;year=-30" aria-label="Arezzo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Gaius Maecenas"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5732 3821h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5732 3821h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.46,11.88&amp;year=-30">What was here in 30 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
